      model:"Smartphones have become part of everyday life, and many children now get their first phone at a very young age. Some people believe that children should wait until they are teenagers before they are allowed to own one. I largely agree with this view.\n\nThe main reason is that smartphones can harm young children's health and development. Many children spend hours every day watching videos or playing games, which can damage their eyesight and reduce the time they spend playing outside. Using a phone late at night also makes it difficult to fall asleep, so children may feel tired at school. In addition, the internet contains a lot of content that is not suitable for children, and young users may not understand the risks of talking to strangers online. Social media can also make children compare themselves with others, which may affect their confidence.\n\nHowever, I accept that phones can be useful in some situations. Parents may want to contact their children when they travel to school alone, and some educational apps can help children learn. In my opinion, the solution is to give younger children a simple phone that can only make calls and send messages. This allows parents to keep their children safe without exposing them to the dangers of the internet. Parents could also set clear rules, such as no phones at the dinner table.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that children should not have smartphones until they are old enough to use them responsibly, although a basic phone for safety reasons is acceptable."
