      model:"Zoos have existed for hundreds of years, but people disagree about whether they should continue. Some argue that keeping animals in zoos is cruel, while others believe that zoos play an important role in protecting wildlife. This essay will discuss both views.\n\nOn the one hand, many people believe zoos are unkind to animals. Large animals such as elephants and tigers travel long distances in the wild, but in a zoo they often live in small spaces. As a result, some of them show signs of stress, such as walking backwards and forwards for hours. Critics also say that animals in zoos cannot behave naturally, because they do not need to hunt or find food.\n\nOn the other hand, supporters argue that modern zoos help to save endangered species. Some animals, such as certain types of rhino, might already be extinct without breeding programmes in zoos. Zoos also have an educational value. When children see real animals, they become more interested in nature and more likely to support conservation in the future. In addition, scientists can study animals in zoos to learn how to protect them better in the wild.\n\nIn my opinion, zoos should not be closed, but they must improve. Animals should have large enclosures that are similar to their natural habitats, and zoos should focus on endangered species rather than simply entertaining visitors. Governments should also inspect zoos regularly to make sure that animals are treated well.\n\nIn conclusion, although some zoos are cruel, well-managed zoos can protect animals and educate the public, so I believe they should remain open."
